1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,148
There are two ways to attach a claim to
code, and two ways to embed a language in
a prover.

2
00:00:06,148 --> 00:00:07,837
Both distinctions matter.

3
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:12,076
Intrinsic specification puts the claim in
the type.

4
00:00:12,076 --> 00:00:18,494
Here the caller supplies evidence that the
list is not empty, so the function has no
empty case.

5
00:00:18,494 --> 00:00:24,109
Illegal states become unrepresentable, and
every caller gets the guarantee for free.

6
00:00:24,109 --> 00:00:29,591
The cost is that types get heavier, and
evidence gets threaded through signatures.

7
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:43,522
Extrinsic specification writes the plain
program, then states a separate theorem
about it. double is just addition, and the
theorem says it equals two times its
argument.

8
00:00:43,522 --> 00:00:48,985
The program stays idiomatic, and the
specification can grow by accretion.

9
00:00:48,985 --> 00:00:52,952
The cost is that you have to find the
right theorems.

10
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:55,413
A rule of thumb.

11
00:00:55,413 --> 00:01:02,737
Use intrinsic specification for shape:
lengths, non-emptiness, invariants of a
data structure.

12
00:01:02,737 --> 00:01:12,087
Use extrinsic specification for behaviour:
what a sort returns, what a file contains
after a write, what a protocol did.

13
00:01:13,200 --> 00:01:14,813
There is a second axis.

14
00:01:14,813 --> 00:01:23,370
A deep embedding makes the language under
study into data inside the prover, with a
semantics and a theory built about it.

15
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:29,472
Iris does this for concurrent and unsafe
programs; interaction trees do it for
effects.

16
00:01:29,472 --> 00:01:36,136
It buys reusable reasoning about hard
semantics, and it costs you the embedding
and its theory.

17
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:41,762
A shallow embedding does not model the
language; it uses it.

18
00:01:41,762 --> 00:01:44,619
The prover is the programming language.

19
00:01:44,619 --> 00:01:51,506
Terms are programs, types are
specifications, and proofs are terms
checked by the same kernel.

20
00:01:51,506 --> 00:01:56,854
There is no gap between the code you
reason about and the code that runs.

21
00:01:57,967 --> 00:02:00,534
This tutorial takes the shallow path.

22
00:02:00,534 --> 00:02:04,419
Next, what happens to the proofs when the
code compiles.

